.\" SPDX-License-Identifier: BSD-2-Clause
.Dd October 10, 2026
.Dt LIBFIDL_BSD 3
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm libfidl_bsd
.Nd experimental native FIDL bindings over capability channels
.Sh LIBRARY
Native FIDL library
.Pq libfidl_bsd, Fl lfidl_bsd
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.In fidl/bsd/runtime_c.h
.Ft int
.Fn fidl_bsd_connection_create "int fd" "enum fidl_bsd_role role" "struct fidl_bsd_connection **result"
.Ft uint32_t
.Fn fidl_bsd_runtime_abi_version "void"
.Ft int
.Fn fidl_bsd_connection_create_with_options "int fd" "enum fidl_bsd_role role" "const struct fidl_bsd_options *options" "struct fidl_bsd_connection **result"
.Ft int
.Fn fidl_bsd_connection_peer_epitaph "const struct fidl_bsd_connection *connection" "int32_t *status"
.Ft void
.Fn fidl_bsd_connection_destroy "struct fidl_bsd_connection *connection"
.Ft void
.Fn fidl_bsd_connection_close "struct fidl_bsd_connection *connection" "int error"
.Ft int
.Fn fidl_bsd_connection_poll "struct fidl_bsd_connection *connection" "int timeout_ms"
.Ft int
.Fn fidl_bsd_connection_dispatch "struct fidl_bsd_connection *connection"
.Ft int
.Fn fidl_bsd_connection_fd "const struct fidl_bsd_connection *connection"
.Ft int
.Fn fidl_bsd_connection_wants_write "const struct fidl_bsd_connection *connection"
.Ft int
.Fn fidl_bsd_connection_error "const struct fidl_bsd_connection *connection"
.Ft int
.Fn fidl_bsd_connection_cancel "struct fidl_bsd_connection *connection" "uint32_t txid"
.Ft int
.Fn fidl_bsd_connection_shutdown "struct fidl_bsd_connection *connection" "int32_t epitaph"
.Ft void
.Fn fidl_bsd_request_retain "struct fidl_bsd_request *request"
.Ft void
.Fn fidl_bsd_request_release "struct fidl_bsd_request *request"
.Ft int
.Fn fidl_bsd_take_fd "int *fd"
.Ft "const struct channel_message *"
.Fn fidl_bsd_request_native "const struct fidl_bsd_request *request"
.Sh DESCRIPTION
The
.Nm
library combines upstream FIDL natural types and codecs with
.Xr libchannel 3 .
Generated C++ client and server adapters use
.Vt fidl::bsd::Connection ;
generated C interfaces use the opaque types in the synopsis.
C is the primary application interface.
The C bridge is implemented in C++ but its public headers and application
callbacks compile as C.
No Zircon kernel, syscall emulation, or Fuchsia dispatcher is used.
.Ss Status
This is an experimental source-tree backend.
Native library, header, and manual installation targets are registered.
C11 and C++ consumers pass against a staged shared-library installation.
Production service migration and full world/package validation remain open.
The standalone build produces
.Pa /usr/obj/5bsd-fidl-runtime/libfidl_bsd.a .
See
.Xr fidl 7
for the integration's scope and remaining work.
.Ss Building applications
Installed public headers live under
.Pa /usr/include/fidl/bsd ;
pinned support headers live under
.Pa /usr/include/fidl/support .
Use
.Cm pkgconf --cflags --libs fidl
to obtain compiler and linker flags.
Add
.Fl -static
to pkgconf for private dependencies when linking the static runtime archive.
Generated natural types and the generated C bridge compile as C++20.
Application sources can compile as C11 and use the C linker with those flags.
The runtime installs as
.Pa libfidl_bsd.so.1
and
.Pa libfidl_bsd.a .
The optional host SDK provides
.Xr fidlgen-bsd 1 .
.Ss Connection lifecycle
.Fn fidl_bsd_connection_create
takes ownership of the endpoint descriptor on success only.
Failed creation clears the result pointer.
.Fn fidl_bsd_connection_create_with_options
accepts explicit resource limits in a sized
.Vt struct fidl_bsd_options
initialized with
.Dv FIDL_BSD_OPTIONS_INITIALIZER .
Unknown sizes or nonzero reserved fields return
.Er EINVAL .
.Fn fidl_bsd_runtime_abi_version
returns
.Dv FIDL_BSD_ABI_VERSION .
Use
.Dv FIDL_BSD_CLIENT
or
.Dv FIDL_BSD_SERVER
for the role.
Each connection has one dispatch thread.
.Fn fidl_bsd_connection_poll
waits through kqueue and dispatches messages;
its timeout is in milliseconds, with zero for a poll and a negative value
for an indefinite wait.
An existing event loop may instead register the borrowed connection fd,
monitor write readiness while
.Fn fidl_bsd_connection_wants_write
is nonzero, and call
.Fn fidl_bsd_connection_dispatch .
.Pp
Closing from a callback is supported.
Destroying the connection object from its own callback is not supported.
Pending calls complete with an error on cancellation or peer death.
.Fn fidl_bsd_connection_destroy
first closes the connection, so pending calls complete with
.Er ECANCELED
while the handle is still valid; those callbacks may query or close it.
Cancellation is local and does not undo remote work.
Replies are checked against both the FIDL transaction ID and method ordinal
after native reply-token matching.
Transaction IDs are never reused on a connection; reconnect on exhaustion.
.Pp
A server may begin protocol shutdown with
.Fn fidl_bsd_connection_shutdown .
It queues an epitaph and refuses new sends and replies.
Keep polling until the peer closes, or enforce an application deadline and
close locally.
A successful send does not guarantee delivery; immediate destruction may
discard queued output.
.Fn fidl_bsd_connection_peer_epitaph
returns the received signed protocol status, or
.Er ENOENT
before an epitaph arrives.
.Ss Descriptors and buffers
Descriptors travel as native channel attachments.
An integer in message bytes is not authority.
The expected type comes from the generated schema.
The message body carries a presence marker, not a descriptor-type label.
The receiver validates the actual kernel attachment against its schema;
it does not trust peer-supplied type or rights claims.
Generic descriptors have no narrower object-type constraint.
Checking a channel endpoint's object type does not prove that the peer
implements the named protocol.
.Pp
The C++ descriptor wrapper is move-only and consumes outgoing descriptor
fields even when encoding or sending fails.
Descriptor zero is valid.
.Pp
Descriptor checks are made by the message system calls: the sender's
expectations are checked and applied by the kernel before a message is
accepted, moved descriptors are closed by the kernel, and the receiver
validates against the kernel's report of each attachment, as described in
.Xr libchannel 3 .
.Pp
The C interface borrows outgoing buffers and descriptors and sends a private
duplicate of each, which is delivered with close-on-exec and close-on-fork
locked.
The caller retains its original descriptor and rights.
A private duplicate preserves kernel transfer restrictions.
Sending a descriptor restricted with
.Dv CAP_XFER_NONE
fails with
.Er ENOTCAPABLE ;
a non-passable object such as a kqueue fails with
.Er EINVAL .
Immediate send failure preserves the C caller's original descriptor and
returns an error without invoking a two-way call's callback.
Accepted queued output can fail later during dispatch.
.Pp
Decoded C callback buffers last until callback return.
Callback descriptors are also released on return unless taken with
.Fn fidl_bsd_take_fd ,
which returns the descriptor and replaces its field with \-1.
Take a descriptor before closing or retaining it, and copy a string before
retaining its bytes.
.Pp
A server callback may retain its request context for a deferred reply using
.Fn fidl_bsd_request_retain
and later release it with
.Fn fidl_bsd_request_release .
This does not extend the lifetime of decoded callback buffers.
The context exposes its original kernel-stamped message through
.Fn fidl_bsd_request_native .
Application authorization remains the provider's responsibility.
.Ss Supported bindings
The C/C++ adapters support closed, ajar, and open protocols, with strict or
flexible methods and events as permitted by the FIDL compiler.
Payload types may be named, anonymous, local, or imported.
Payload-free transactions carry only their header.
The C projection supports local and imported structs, tables, unions,
enums/bits,
nested arrays and vectors, optional values, descriptors, and protocol endpoints.
Strict application-error replies expose a generated result union;
the callback errno argument reports transport or conversion failure.
Dependent libraries reuse the declaring library's C types and private
inline conversion support.
Use the repeated
.Fl -dependency-json
option of
.Xr fidlgen-bsd 1
to supply their compiler IR.
Composed protocol methods preserve their original ordinals and payload types.
.Ss Protocol evolution
Closed protocols reject unknown interactions.
Ajar protocols accept unknown flexible one-way methods and events.
Open protocols also answer unknown flexible two-way methods with the standard
FIDL unknown-method result.
The generated client reports
.Er EOPNOTSUPP
without closing the connection; the C callback receives a null result pointer.
Application errors remain separate from callback errno status.
Unknown strict interactions and malformed messages close the connection.
.Pp
Open/ajar C server and event structures have an optional
.Va unknown_interaction
callback receiving the ordinal, plus a two-way flag for servers.
C++ handlers provide
.Fn OnUnknownMethod
and
.Fn OnUnknownEvent .
These hooks receive no unknown payload or descriptors.
The runtime closes unknown attachments.
.Pp
Known methods tolerate a different strict/flexible header flag.
A generated client installs a default event handler if none exists:
known events are decoded and discarded, while unknown events follow the
protocol openness rules.
In C this occurs on the first generated client call; bind event handlers
explicitly to receive events before making a call.
An explicitly bound event handler takes precedence.
.Ss C aggregate layouts
Strings and vectors use pointer/size pairs.
Optional strings and vectors also have a
.Li _present
flag; an absent outgoing value must have a null pointer and zero size.
Arrays are inline C arrays.
Boxed structs and optional unions use nullable pointers.
Tables have
.Li has_MEMBER
flags, and unions have
.Li tag
and
.Li value
fields with generated tag constants.
An unknown flexible union is exposed using the generated unknown-tag constant;
its discarded unknown data cannot be re-encoded.
Enum/bits types use fixed-width integer typedefs and prefixed constants.
.Pp
Nested callback buffers have the same borrowed lifetime as top-level buffers.
Use
.Fn fidl_bsd_take_fd
to retain a nested descriptor.
Conversion bounds recursion, allocation work, and descriptor count.
Cyclic boxed input fails with
.Er EINVAL .
Failure to allocate a C response view completes the callback once with
.Er ENOMEM
and releases unclaimed descriptors.
.Pp
C++ endpoint wrappers preserve their protocol and direction in their type.
C endpoint fields are descriptors with the same ownership rules as handles.
Absent optional endpoints use \-1 in C.
Keyword and helper-field collisions are escaped with a deterministic
.Li _fidl
suffix.
Unsupported schemas fail generation.
.Pp
Supported handle subtypes map to generic native descriptors, sockets, and
capability channels.
READ and WRITE have explicit Capsicum mappings; SAME_RIGHTS preserves
existing attenuation.
Socket type checks need fstat rights; explicit socket rights preserve
CAP_FSTAT for that check.
Channel type checks need GETINFO permission and currently require SAME_RIGHTS.
The kernel continues to enforce transfer ceilings, revocation, and locked
exec and fork restrictions.
.Sh RETURN VALUES
Status functions return errno values directly, with zero for success.
.Fn fidl_bsd_connection_fd
returns a borrowed descriptor, or \-1 for an invalid connection.
.Fn fidl_bsd_take_fd
returns the taken descriptor, or \-1 if absent.
.Sh FILES
.Bl -tag -width indent
.It Pa /usr/src/lib/libfidl/README
Build, ownership, supported schema, and validation details.
.It Pa /usr/src/tools/build/fuchsia/generate-bsd.py
Generate native adapters; the --c option also emits C interfaces.
.It Pa /usr/src/tools/build/fuchsia/build-runtime.py
Build and test the native runtime, including the sanitizer variant.
.El
.Sh ERRORS
.Bl -tag -width EOPNOTSUPP
.It Er EINVAL
Invalid argument or failed encoding constraint.
.It Er EPROTO
Malformed or unexpected protocol message.
.It Er ENOTCAPABLE
Insufficient descriptor rights or forbidden transfer.
.It Er EFTYPE
Wrong descriptor type.
.It Er ENOBUFS
Native pending-call or outgoing-queue limit reached.
.It Er EMSGSIZE
The message exceeds native byte or descriptor limits.
.It Er EOVERFLOW
Transaction IDs are exhausted; create a new connection.
.It Er EALREADY
A request is already completed, cancelled, or replied to.
.It Er ESHUTDOWN
The server has begun protocol shutdown.
.It Er EOPNOTSUPP
A descriptor type or rights mapping is unsupported.
.El
Other native transport and allocation errors are propagated.
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr fidlgen-bsd 1 ,
.Xr kqueue 2 ,
.Xr libchannel 3 ,
.Xr libservice 3 ,
.Xr capsicum 4 ,
.Xr fidl 7
